{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs at exact clean pushed head 964ffdb53e6b584302d0a2bf8c210b75afc5cc22, branch request/fix-resident-doc-command, restoring the runnable deploy-a-resident command so main CI can go green. Fulfils request f76bc5b7 under promise a005291b.\n\nTHE CHANGE. One line in docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md, one insertion and one deletion, no other file and no unrelated prose:\n\n  -git update-ref -d refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e\n  +git -C \"$REPO\" update-ref -d \"refs/gitseq/resident/$GENESIS\"\n\nBoth halves are load-bearing. `\u003cgenesis\u003e` is not a placeholder to a shell: `\u003c` opens an input redirection and the trailing `\u003e` opens an output redirection with no target, so the file does not parse and nothing in the page runs. And the command needed `-C \"$REPO\"`, because internal/docset/examples_test.go joins a page's sh blocks into one script and runs it with `cd \"$SCRATCH\"`, while this page's REPO is `$(mktemp -d)/project`, somewhere else entirely; a bare `git` would have parsed but operated on the wrong tree, or on no repository at all. GENESIS is already established on the page from `gs status --json`, so the corrected form invents nothing.\n\nI checked the second condition the request set, that the command still be the one an operator should actually run. It is: freeing a stale claim is exactly `update-ref -d` on that ref, and naming the repository explicitly is what a reader following the page would need anyway. I also confirmed `git update-ref -d` exits 0 on a ref that does not exist, so the block stays runnable whether or not a claim was ever made.\n\nEVIDENCE FOR THE CAUSE, WHICH I DID NOT TAKE ON TRUST AND WHICH DID NOT GO SMOOTHLY. I could not reproduce the failure locally. With the broken line restored on this machine the focused gate passes, repeatedly, in about five seconds; I confirmed with a parser probe that the broken block really is inside the executed script, and confirmed in isolation that bash rejects that exact text with a syntax error. Rather than ship a fix for a cause I could not observe, I read the CI log for run 32562376446, which says precisely this:\n\n  example.sh: line 26: syntax error near unexpected token `newline'\n  --- FAIL: TestGateDocumentedCommandsRun/docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md\n  documented commands failed: exit status 2\n\nThat is the cause codex named and the one this change removes. The local anomaly is unexplained and I am recording it rather than burying it: on this machine a green run of that page proves nothing, so no reviewer should treat a local pass as evidence the page is sound. Working out why is separate work; it does not affect this head.\n\nGATES at the exact head, all run, none inferred. Focused uncached TestGateDocumentedCommandsRun: ok, internal/docset 20.782s. make docs: ok, 45.250s. make test: no failing package. git diff --check: clean. Worktree clean, branch pushed.\n\nScope is documentation-only; no Go source is touched.","body":{"branch":"request/fix-resident-doc-command","commit":"964ffdb53e6b584302d0a2bf8c210b75afc5cc22","path":"docs","tests":"go test -count=1 -run TestGateDocumentedCommandsRun ./internal/docset/ (20.782s); make docs (45.250s); make test; git diff --check"}}